3. Public source required for modification and code reuse

If you modify the Covered Material, or copy or incorporate any
copyrightable portion of its source into another program, website, or
service, you must publish the complete corresponding source of the
resulting combined or derived work under this same license BEFORE its
first use, execution, deployment, or distribution. This requirement applies
even to private or internal use and to services accessed over a network;
it is not limited to binary distribution. Existing independent programs
merely stored alongside the work are not thereby covered.

Corresponding source means the preferred editable form of the work and
its modifications, together with build and installation scripts,
dependency/version information, and other material needed to understand
and rebuild it. Publish it in a publicly accessible repository, without
payment, account registration, or access approval. Keep the exact source
for every distributed or deployed version available while that version is
offered or used, and for at least three years after its last distribution
or deployment. Include a conspicuous source URL in distributions and
network-service interfaces.

Do not publish private signing keys, tokens, passwords, personal records,
or unrelated private data. Provide documented configuration placeholders
and a build path using the recipient's own credentials where needed.
Publishing the source does NOT permit commercial use.

